A||||15||Nashco||Have you reprogrammed your ECM?||||5||
Z||000000||Nashco||05-16-2001||07:54 PM||||I'm just starting to dig into my new priority three project, learning to reprogram my own ECM.  I have a guinea pig ECM I'm going to use, and have a few steps I need to get to before actually programming.<P>Has anybody out there done this on their own, as I am going to do?  I know 1FST2M6 has, but that's all I know of, and I'm hoping there are some people out there that could give me a few directions to head in.<P>First, where can I get clean (unburned) chips?  Does anybody know what style they are so I could search (Digikey?).  Second, does anybody have a printout, electronic copy, notepad, anything with the parameter code on it?  I have been doing some research lately, but haven't come up with any older ECM or fiero specific stuff.  These are two giant hurdles I'm about to give a shot, and I was hoping I could get a helping hand from some of you out there.<P>I have a few goals with this project, the main one being to learn what is involved in making custom parameters.  I would like to be able to supply the Fiero community with cheaper custom proms (for many different setups like turbo/supercharger, different injectors, cams, etc.) once I get into it, in hopes of getting some valuable experience and connections.  My second biggest goal is to use that experience gained to apply it towards other proms, such as my '89 4.3 v6 ecm that I want to turbo later on down the road.<P>I'm really looking for any help, website links, people to talk to, companies who might share some of their info, electronics supply information, etc.  Anything to help would be great!<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT<P>P.S.  I have the ability to do this, please don't think I'm just testing out the waters to see if I can.  I will do it, but the more help I get the quicker it will happen, and the quicker I will be able to help others in return <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Thanks gang!<P>||||reg||5
Z||000001||88formula||05-16-2001||08:02 PM||||Maybe this website will help you <A HREF="http://www.diy-efi.org" TARGET=_blank>http://www.diy-efi.org</A>  and this one <A HREF="http://www.users.bigpond.com/jack_stands/automotive_advice/fuel_injection/efi_repair_1.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.users.bigpond.com/jack_stands/automotive_advice/fuel_injection/efi_repair_1.html</A> <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Dave Gunsul||05-16-2001||08:04 PM||||Good luck to you Bryce! God knows someone should make a cheaper custom chip.<BR>I emailed Superchips and they said it would<BR>cost $199.00 for the chip and $125.00 for the<BR>custom programming!! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> That's BS!!<BR>That price doesn't even include shipping!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><BR>I guess when you're the only game in town you can charge whatever you want.<P>------------------<BR>Dave Gunsul<BR>N.I.F.E. member<BR>86 GT modified<BR>85 SE daily driver||||reg||1
Z||000003||Cooter||05-16-2001||09:18 PM||||The chip you need will depend on which ECM you are messing with. Mine uses 27c128 (GM '749 ecm), but the Fiero uses a 2732. I am learning about chip tuning and having a lot of fun doing it. I got the eraser and programmer from xtronics and the chips from Jameco. The actual tuning software is from one of the members of the Forum- I know that he will read this and will probably respond to you personally if you are interested in his software package. So far, I have made a couple of chips to disable VATS and alter the cooling fans for a couple of TPI v8's and screw around with the fuel curve on the Fiero. I am not messing with it too much until I get the EGT gauge mounted because I'd hate to roast the darling little 3.4 I have. The diy-efi website is great for reading and the 3rd gen forum has some info too. Happy reading!||||reg||1
Z||000004||Will||05-17-2001||06:48 PM||||Bump||||reg||1
Z||000005||Nashco||05-18-2001||12:46 AM||||Yes, bump indeed Will <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Thanks for the links 88formula.  I had the diy-efi one, it is a huge resource.  Obviously, I'm hunting through that one at every chance I get...there is soo much information.<P>Cooter, what year/engine/tranny are you dealing with?  I have an 88 5 speed 2.8 ECM, not that this is a issue for me right now, I am looking for total basics right now.  So, 2732 is the chip style, correct?  So you got the EPROM pocket programmer kit?  I see that kit doesn't require any adapters for the 2732, and for another 50 bucks you can get the timer UV eraser.  Does that mean you can reuse the chips, by just exposing them and reburning them?<P>I haven't got any email from the mystery person with the software...can you email me at nashco@hotmail.com please with more info of who this is?  Actually, if you would be willing to give me your phone number, I would love to talk on Friday or Sunday night about some of this Cooter.<P>What's VATS?  I'm thinking it's like..vehicle active traction system.  That must be N* stuff...?  I wish I had a TPI with the easily tuneable computer and all the info <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  It's better that I do this for the Fiero though...get some good basis to work from when working with more advanced systems.<P>Anybody have some more to add?  I really hope to get some solid info on this, so that I can help teach others to do the same.  This technology isn't super-complex by any means, we just have to decipher everything and spread what's the best thing to do!  Keep the info coming guys!<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000006||mrfixit58||05-18-2001||08:37 AM||||Actually, VATS stands for Vehicle Anti-Theft System. That's the system that requires a chip in the ignition key to start the vehicle.<P>Free bump.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR><P>------------------<BR>Roy :D<BR>87 GT  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pennocksize.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Jay||05-18-2001||05:37 PM||||Very interesting, especially for us Northstar guys!  Bump, Bump <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Jay<BR>1986 Northstar GT<BR>www.members.home.net/jaygrande||||reg||1
Z||000008||Nashco||05-18-2001||07:20 PM||||Damn...I guessed wrong <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  Just as a side note, I wonder what's involved in having the resistor chip key tumbler.  GM vehicles are notorious for having similar steering column apparatus...I wonder if there's a swap you could do to include it.  That's a nice feature for anti-theft...to keep the rookie car thieves limited to window breaking and no car <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>More research on the net will come in the next week...I'll probably have several questions to post.<P>Cooter, you out there?<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000009||Cooter||05-18-2001||08:12 PM||||I am here- I am running my 88 GT 3.4 with a 91 GMC Syclone '749 ecm, prom and map sensor. I did this for a couple of reasons- Eventually, I will be running a supercharger and the '749 does good with the positive pressure manifold. Also, my old ecm died and I was told that I could not make the car work with a newer style ecm, so it was also a matter of pride. At work, we are building some pre 50's hotrods and putting tuned port 350's in them. The local guys wanted up to $200 to disable the VATS and reset the cooling fans to come on at a reasonable temperature. Because of all the above reasons, I just decided to start learning what to do on the ecms to make them work in my particular application. Some of the software sets that I played with were Tunercat, Winbin, programmer98 (specifically for the Syclone ecm) and GMEPro. Of all the different programs, I really like the GMEpro the best. It works on my old DOS computer, so I don't tie up the good computer while the wife is home.<BR>On the topic of erasing the chips- Yes and no. They will only take a good erasing a few times before they will not take the programming again. I have burned up several so far, but now I have a really good chip that helped to smooth the car out.||||reg||1
Z||000010||terryk||05-18-2001||10:17 PM||||A cheap anti-theft is to put a switch on the fuel pump.<P>True VATs requires the ECM to have an input for it. The Fiero's don't.<p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 05-18-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||Taijiguy||05-18-2001||10:27 PM||||Actually, the VATs is pretty easy to bypass if you have the original key. All it is, is a resistor embeded into the key.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000012||Will||05-18-2001||11:10 PM||||For VATS, thwe resistor in the key is used to complete an RC oscillator circuit which oscillates at a specific frequency. Before cranking, the ECM looks for a square wave within certain frequency window, which has changed a couple of times over the years. We've built and tested a couple of 555 based oscillators which when fed to the ECM allow us to bypass VATS.<BR>I don't know much about PassKey right now.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000013||terryk||05-19-2001||11:31 AM||||Passkey has a few versions, but it learns the key/resistor unlike VATS that just had the oscillator input to the ECM. Something like 2000 combinations although they tended to use values under 11k ohms.<p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 05-19-2001).]||||reg||1
